          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 14,
          "ms": 20
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The term species can be defined using different concepts, including the biological species concept and the morphological species concept.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 14
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The morphological species concept is based on appearance or observable characteristics.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 14
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Describe what is meant by the biological species concept.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 14
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 1,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "18.1(1)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 1,
          "instruction": null,
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "if individuals can breed to produce fertile offspring <b>;</b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "The biological species concept concerns interbreeding and fertile offspring. Morphological similarity alone may not establish membership of the same biological species.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:18.1(1)",
                "ms:b#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Identify the species concept being used.",
            "State the relevant reproductive or ecological criterion."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
              "text": "Defining a biological species only by similar appearance",
              "basis": "inferred",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:18.1(1)"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "type": "short"
      },
      {
        "key": "c",
        "label": "(c)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 15,
          "ms": 20
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Populations of <i>E. eschscholtzii</i> are separated by the Central Valley, shown in Fig. 6.2, which contains conditions unsuitable for salamanders.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 15
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "<b> </b> <b> </b> Some scientists think that the separated populations of <i>E. eschscholtzii </i>have evolved to form different species of salamander.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 15
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "figure",
            "id": "fig6.2",
            "caption": "Fig. 6.2",
            "src": "figures/q6_fig6_2.png",
            "alt": "Distribution of salamanders in California",
            "source": {
              "qp": 15
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "<b> </b> <b> </b> Individuals from different salamander populations may not be able to reproduce with each other, even if they are able to interact.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 15
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Explain how salamanders from different populations have evolved to be unable to reproduce with each other.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 15
            }
          }
        ],
        "marks": 3,
        "topics": [
          {
            "ref": "17.3(3)",
            "role": "primary"
          }
        ],
        "markScheme": {
          "marks": 3,
          "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i> <i> </i>",
          "guidance": [],
          "points": [
            {
              "n": 1,
              "text": "<b>1</b>  allopatric speciation <b>; </b> <b> </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 2,
              "text": "<b>2</b>  <u>geographical</u>, isolation / barrier / separation <b>;   </b> <b> </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 3,
              "text": "<b>3</b>  (so) no gene flow (between populations) <b>; </b> <b> </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 4,
              "text": "<b>4</b>  (populations have) different, mutations / advantageous alleles / alleles increase in frequency <b>;</b> <b> </b> <b> </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            },
            {
              "n": 5,
              "text": "<b>5</b>  (populations have) different, selection pressures / environments <b>; </b>",
              "numberedInMs": false
            }
          ]
        },
        "er": [],
        "aiSupplement": {
          "explanation": [
            {
              "text": "Isolation reduces gene flow. Mutation, selection and drift can then cause divergence until reproductive isolation remains even if populations come into contact again.",
              "refs": [
                "syllabus:17.3(3)",
                "ms:c#1"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reasoningChain": [
            "Identify the barrier to gene flow.",
            "Connect genetic divergence to the formation of reproductively isolated species."
          ],
          "inferredMistakes": [
            {
